window.docContents[230].resolve({"/References/#Type-Systems--References___-Typing-Mutable-References--Properties--Well-Typed-Stores":{"contents":"Since we have extended both the reduction relation (with\ninitial and final stores) and the typing relation (with a store\ntyping), we need some way of relating the two.  Consider trying\nto state progress using only the two relations as they stand,\nwith no connection between ρ and δ: this is wrong:\n\n\n\ntheorem progress_wrong (δ : StoreTy) (t : Tm) (τ : Ty) (ρ : Store)\n    (hTy : <{ ∅ / δ ⊢ t ⦂ τ }>) :\n    (t.IsValue ∨ ∃ t' ρ', t / ρ ⟶ t' / ρ') := by\n\n\nThe trouble is that hTy says nothing about the actual store\nρ we're reducing against.  For example, take δ[0] = Nat, so\nthat !(loc 0) is well typed at type Nat; but take ρ to be\nthe empty store, so that ρ[0] is undefined.  Then !(loc 0)\nis stuck: it isn't a value, and it can't take a step either,\nsince there's no location 0 in ρ to look up.  We need to\nrule this out by requiring that ρ actually satisfies the\nassumptions δ makes about it.  We say that a store ρ is\nwell typed with respect to a store typing δ if the term at\neach location l in ρ has the type at location l in δ.\nSince only closed terms ever get stored in locations (why?), it\nsuffices to type them in the empty context. The following\ndefinition of StoreWellTyped formalizes this:\n\n\n\ndef StoreWellTyped (δ:StoreTy) (ρ:Store) :=\n  δ.length = ρ.length ∧\n  (∀ (l : Nat), l < ρ.length → <{ ∅ / δ ⊢ ~(ρ[l]) ⦂ ~(δ[l]) }>)\n\n\nInformally, we will write δ ⊢ ρ for StoreWellTyped δ ρ.\n\nscoped notation:40 δ:41 \" ⊢ \" ρ:41 => StoreWellTyped δ ρ\n\n\nIntuitively, a store ρ is consistent with a store typing\nδ if every value in the store has the type predicted by the\nstore typing.  The only subtle point is the fact that, when typing\nthe values in the store, we supply the very same store typing to\nthe typing relation.  This allows us to type circular stores like\nthe one we saw above.\n\nCan you find a store ρ, and two\ndifferent store typings δ₁ and δ₂ such that both\nδ₁ ⊢ ρ and δ₂ ⊢ ρ?theorem store_not_unique:\n  exists ρ, exists δ₁, exists δ₂,\n    δ₁ ⊢ ρ ∧ δ₂ ⊢ ρ ∧ δ₁ ≠ δ₂ := by\n  sorry\n\n\n","context":"Type Systems\u0009References: Typing Mutable References\u0009Properties","header":"9.5.1. Well-Typed Stores","id":"/References/#Type-Systems--References___-Typing-Mutable-References--Properties--Well-Typed-Stores"}});